# Exercise: >aux and aux>
#
# Sometimes a value is in your way: you'd like the main stack
# free for the next operation, but you can't drop the value —
# you still need it later. The aux stack solves this.
#
#   >aux    moves the top of the main stack to the aux stack
#   aux>    moves the top of the aux stack back to the main stack
#
# Every colon-defined word gets its own private aux stack. It
# starts empty when the word begins and is discarded when the
# word returns, so aux can't leak between words.
#
# Why bother, when `pick`, `roll`, and friends can reach any
# depth? Because some operations (like calls into library words
# you'll meet later) want the main stack arranged a particular
# way. Stashing intermediate values to aux lets you keep the
# main stack clean for those operations without juggling.
#
# Example: hold one value while you process the others.
#
#   ( a b c )
#   swap >aux     ( a c )      aux: [b]
#   i.+           ( a+c )      aux: [b]
#   aux>          ( a+c b )    aux: []
#
# Your task: given ( 10 20 30 ) on the stack, produce ( 40 20 )
# by stashing the middle value to aux, summing the other two,
# then pulling the stashed value back.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-aux ( -- )
    10 20 30
    # Your code here — replace the next line with the real answer
    3drop 0 0

    # Do not edit below this line
    20 test.assert-eq
    40 test.assert-eq
;
